## Why This Is Asked
Tests planning of safe, multi-region rollout with feature flags and clear rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Progressive rollout
- Feature flags
- Telemetry and SLO-aligned signals
- Cache invalidation/versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for rollout gating
const enabled = getFlag("new_api_behavior");
if (enabled && regions.includes(currentRegion)) {
  useNewBehavior();
} else {
  useOldBehavior();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this if some regions have mandatory latency budgets?
- How would you integrate with an incident response playbook?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design isolation, backpressure controls, and controlled degradation under burst traffic, with clear rollback criteria and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation via edge throttling and per-tenant queues
- Backpressure mechanisms and circuit breakers
- Safe degradation strategies and cache fallbacks
- Telemetry: p95/p99 latency, error rate, queue depth, saturation
- Clear rollback criteria and automation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple token bucket limiter
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) {
      this.tokens -= 1;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate plan with load tests and synthetic surges?
- What metrics and thresholds would trigger a rollback, and how would you automate it?

Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical reliability strategy for multi-tenant, geo-distributed services under sudden load, balancing fairness and SLA adherence.

Key Concepts

- QoS with quotas and burst budgets
- Ingress-level enforcement and sidecar orchestration
- Dynamic rate limiting and circuit breakers
- Cross-region traffic steering and graceful degradation
- Observability: SLI/SLO dashboards, latency tails, queue depth
- Rollout/rollback: staged changes, synthetic tests, rapid revert

Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, fillRate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.fillRate = fillRate; // tokens per second
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n = 1) {
    this._inject();
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
  _inject() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.last = now;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.fillRate);
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure and trigger a regional SLA breach, and what would your kill-switch look like?
- What data would you surface in dashboards to operators during a flash spike?

## Why This Is Asked
Probes real-world SRE orchestration for multi-tenant systems under burst traffic, requiring dynamic budgets, fairness, and controlled degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Fusion of signals: latency, error rate, queue depth
- Admission control and load shedding
- Safe rollback plan with tenant-level validation
- Observability and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for tenant budget enforcement
function allocate(tenantId, req) {
  const budget = getBudget(tenantId);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) return reject(req, "budget exhausted");
  // consume
  budget.consume(1);
  return forward(req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate budget fairness under mixed workloads?
- How would you test the load-shedding policy without impacting real tenants?

